    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Old Turkic, the earliest recorded Turkic language, was first written in the 8th century on the stone monuments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> in the Orkhon valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, in an alphabet resembling runes.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, who succeeded the Turks on the steppe and settled in the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, wrote a large literature in it, Buddhist, Manichaean and Christian, much of it recovered from the sands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and the caves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</li><li>The Turkic languages, which spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, are spoken today by some 200 million people, who honour the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a> as the beginning of their written history.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The memorial stone of the Turkic prince Kul Tigin, raised in 732, carries an inscription in Old Turkic runes on three sides and, on the fourth, a Chinese text composed by the Tang emperor Xuanzong.</p></aside>
    </section>

