    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan.jpg" alt="Bilge Qaghan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge Qaghan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_8th_Century_Marble_Statue_of_Bilge_Khan_(35580731981).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Turkic languages form a family of some three dozen closely related languages, spoken today from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their common ancestor was probably spoken on the steppes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and southern Siberia in the last centuries BCE. Some scholars have grouped them with the Mongolic and Tungusic languages in an “Altaic” family, but most linguists today explain the similarities among these languages as the result of long contact rather than common descent.</p><p>The name Türk first appears in history with the state founded in 552 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a>, who rebelled against his overlords, the Rouran, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> mountains. Within a few decades the Turkic Khaganate stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and its rulers exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and China. They did not yet write their own language: the earliest known inscription of the khaganate, at Bugut in Mongolia, from the 580s, is written in Sogdian, the language of the Iranian merchants of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> who served as the scribes and diplomats of the steppe.</p><p>After a period of Chinese domination, the Turks restored their independence in 682 under Ilterish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a>, centred on the Orkhon valley and the sacred forest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otuken">Ötüken</a> in Mongolia. Its rulers, Ilterish, his brother Qapaghan, his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge</a> Khagan and Kul Tigin, and their wise counsellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonyukuk">Tonyukuk</a>, raised great stone monuments inscribed in their own language, in a script of their own, between about 720 and 735.</p><p>These inscriptions, the oldest substantial texts in any Turkic language, are the starting point of Old Turkic. The language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, who overthrew the Turks in 744 and built their own khaganate in Mongolia, was closely related, and their writings carry the history of Old Turkic forward for several centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions.jpg" alt="Orkhon inscriptions" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kultigin_Monument_of_Orkhon_Inscriptions.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vezirtonyukuk, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Turkic script, often called runic because its angular letters resemble the runes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, to which it is unrelated, had about thirty-eight signs and was usually written from right to left. It was designed for the sounds of Turkic: many consonants had two different letters, one used with back vowels and one with front vowels, reflecting vowel harmony. Its origin is debated; many scholars believe that its letters were developed from the Sogdian alphabet, with some signs perhaps taken from the clan marks, tamgas, of the steppe. Inscriptions in the same script are found in the Yenisei valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, where the Kyrgyz used it, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>Old Turkic grammar has the features that still characterise Turkic languages. It is agglutinative: words are built from a root followed by a chain of suffixes, each with one meaning, so that a single word can express “from our cities” or “they had not been able to come”. It follows vowel harmony, so that the vowels of suffixes change to match those of the root. Its word order puts the verb at the end of the sentence, it uses postpositions rather than prepositions, and it has no grammatical gender.</p><p>Many Old Turkic words are still recognisable in modern Turkic languages: tengri, “sky” or “heaven”, the name of the sky god; suv, “water”; tash, “stone”; and qaghan, the title of the ruler, which, as khan, became famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> adopted the Sogdian alphabet, derived ultimately from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, to write their language; this Old Uyghur script later became the model for the Mongolian script and through it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> script. Old Turkic texts were also written in the scripts of the religions that the Uyghurs followed, the Manichaean, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Indian Brahmi scripts, and from the 10th century Turkic Muslims wrote their language in Arabic script.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="Kashgar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%ABytgah_Mosque,_Kashi_(20230923100109).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N509FZ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The inscriptions of the Orkhon valley speak with the voice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a>. In the monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge</a> Khagan and Kul Tigin, the ruler addresses the Turkic people, recalling how they had lost their state through disunity and the seductions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose words were sweet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> soft, and how heaven and the khagans had restored it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonyukuk">Tonyukuk</a> tells the story of his own deeds as adviser and general.</p><p>The Uyghur Khaganate, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from 744 to 840, continued to use the runic script for its monuments, and in 762 or 763 its ruler adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>. When the Kyrgyz destroyed the khaganate in 840, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> moved south and west, founding kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and in the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, above all the kingdom of Qocho near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>. There, over the following centuries, Uyghur monks, scribes and merchants wrote an extensive literature in Old Turkic, mostly Buddhist but also Manichaean and Christian, along with contracts, letters and administrative documents.</p><p>In the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and the lands around it from the 10th century, became the first Turkic dynasty to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and their language, close to Old Turkic, became a language of Islamic literature written in Arabic script. Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> moved westwards across the steppe: the Oghuz, among whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> rose to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the 11th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> of the western steppe.</p><p>The Uyghurs of Qocho submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> in 1209, and Uyghur scribes served the Mongol empire, whose script was adapted from theirs. Old Uyghur writing continued in parts of western China for centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Buddhist scriptures in it were still being copied in the 17th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig.jpg" alt="Kutadgu Bilig" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QutadughuBiliq_wien_p.10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a> are the first works of Turkic literature: eloquent texts, rich in parallelism and formulas of oral poetry, which combine history, political exhortation and lament. Their opening, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> calls on the Turkic lords and people to hear his words, is one of the best-known passages in Turkic languages today.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> literature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> is large and varied. It includes translations of Buddhist scriptures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Chinese and Tocharian, such as the Golden Light Sutra, and the Maitrisimit, a dramatic work on the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Buddha Maitreya</a>; Manichaean hymns and a confession of sins for the laity; Christian texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>; and the Irk Bitig, a book of divination written in the runic script, found in the library cave of Dunhuang, the only complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in that script.</p><p>The Karakhanid period produced two masterpieces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a>, “the wisdom that brings good fortune”, completed in 1069 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Khass Hajib</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, is a long poem on statecraft and ethics in the form of dialogues between a ruler and his counsellors. Around the 1070s, Mahmud al-Kashgari, a Turkic scholar living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan-lughat-al-turk">Compendium of the Languages of the Turks</a>, a dictionary explaining Turkic words in Arabic, with poems, proverbs and a round map of the Turkic world; it survives in a single manuscript copied in 1266, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><p>These works, written for rulers, monks and scholars, show Old Turkic as a language capable of history, poetry, religion, law and science, centuries before the rise of the great Turkic literatures of the Ottoman and Timurid ages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Turkic was not the direct ancestor of every modern Turkic language, but it was close to their common ancestor, and its texts allow linguists to trace their development. After the 13th century the Turkic languages diverged into the groups spoken today: the Oghuz languages, among them Turkish, Azerbaijani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchak-languages">Kipchak languages</a>, among them Kazakh, Kyrgyz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> and Bashkir; the Karluk languages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>; the Siberian languages, such as Sakha and Tuvan; and Chuvash, the distant descendant of the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>.</p><p>Chagatai, the literary language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from the 15th century, and Ottoman Turkish, written in Arabic script with many Persian and Arabic words, were the great Turkic literary languages of later centuries. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet in 1928, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were made to write in Cyrillic, and after 1991 several independent states, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and Turkmenistan, moved to Latin alphabets, as Kazakhstan has begun to do.</p><p>The Old Turkic inscriptions were long a mystery. In the 18th century travellers reported inscriptions in an unknown script in the Yenisei valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. In 1889 the Russian explorer Nikolai Yadrintsev found the great monuments of the Orkhon valley, and on 15 December 1893 the Danish linguist Vilhelm Thomsen announced the decipherment of their script, beginning with the words for “heaven” and “Turk”; the Russian scholar Wilhelm Radloff published the first translation soon after.</p><p>Turkic words have entered many languages: English has borrowed, often through other languages, words such as horde, from the Turkic word for the khan’s camp, yurt and yogurt, and khan itself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kul-tigin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kul-tigin.jpg" alt="Kül Tigin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kul-tigin">Kül Tigin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_Head_of_Koltegin_Statue_(35324303410).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Turkic marks the beginning of the written history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a>, today some 200 million people in more than a dozen countries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a>, which stand in the Orkhon valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, whose cultural landscape is a World Heritage Site, are revered as national monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and other Turkic countries, and their words are read in schools.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, now divided among libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, St Petersburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, are a treasury of the religious history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, preserving Buddhist, Manichaean and Christian texts lost elsewhere, and show the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> as a meeting place of languages and faiths.</p><p>Mahmud al-Kashgari’s Compendium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Khass Hajib</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a> are honoured as classics of Turkic culture, and the Old Uyghur script lives on in the traditional Mongolian script still used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and revived in Mongolia.</p><p>The spread of Turkic languages from the Orkhon valley to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> is one of the great movements of peoples and languages in world history, and Old Turkic is the window through which historians can see its beginnings.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Old Turkic, the earliest recorded Turkic language, was first written in the 8th century on the runic monuments of the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> in a rich Buddhist, Manichaean and Christian literature, followed by the Islamic works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>. Deciphered in 1893, it marks the beginning of the written history of the Turkic languages, spoken today by some 200 million people.</em></p>

